1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,513
In Module 04, you ran the chain one call at a time, on one pair of images.

2
00:00:05,513 --> 00:00:08,512
A survey has thousands of pairs every night.

3
00:00:08,512 --> 00:00:13,550
So cuPhoton also ships the chain as one pipeline: the transient chain.

4
00:00:14,450 --> 00:00:19,375
One worker per GPU takes each image from its files to a table of candidates.

5
00:00:19,375 --> 00:00:29,075
It reads the planes, puts the reference on the target's grid, matches and subtracts, finds the hotspots, cuts stamps, fits them, and builds an error bar for each.

6
00:00:29,075 --> 00:00:31,962
The images stay on the GPU the whole way.

7
00:00:31,962 --> 00:00:34,575
What comes back is one small download.

8
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:36,963
Two things come back.

9
00:00:36,963 --> 00:00:44,900
A catalog, with one row per candidate: a position, a flux, and an error bar built from several terms, each in its own column.

10
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:53,250
And a summary for every image: which GPU, which reader actually ran, which error terms went in, and how long each stage took.

11
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:57,600
Everything it does is set in one configuration file.

12
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,387
First, where each plane of your data is and what its numbers mean.

13
00:01:01,387 --> 00:01:03,300
Then every stage's settings.

14
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:06,900
It ships with values chosen for another survey's data.

15
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:08,450
Yours is different.

16
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:11,300
So, the question of this module.

17
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:15,975
When the shipped pipeline says it's done, can you trust what it wrote, on your data?

18
00:01:15,975 --> 00:01:18,850
You already have every habit you need to find out.
